[1] |e0+v|^2 = 2*cos(theta) + 2
[2] |e0+v|^2 = 1  <=>  2cos(theta)+2 = 1  <=>  cos(theta) = [-1/2]
[2b] theta in [0,2pi):  [2*pi/3, 4*pi/3]   exact coords: [[-1/2, sqrt(3)/2], [-1/2, -sqrt(3)/2]]
[2c] residual check of the equation at both roots: [0, 0] ; at theta=0,pi (v=+-e0): [3, -1]
[3] v=+120deg: v=[-1/2, sqrt(3)/2], |v|^2=1, |e0+v|^2=1  -> in S? True
[3] v=-120deg: v=[-1/2, -sqrt(3)/2], |v|^2=1, |e0+v|^2=1  -> in S? True
[4] v=-e0 (antiparallel): |e0+v|^2 = 0  -> point x+e0+v = x, distance from x = 0, so NOT in S.
[4b] v=+e0 (parallel): point x+2e0, distance 2, NOT in S.
[5] solutions in [0,2pi) of |e0+v|=|v|=1: exactly the two above; 2cos(theta)+2-1 = 0 has
    exactly two roots mod 2pi since cos is 2pi-periodic and strictly monotone on [0,pi] and [pi,2pi].
[6] per-direction Jacobian mass  int_{|e+v|<=h} |sin(arg v - arg e)| dv = h**2   (expect h^2 exactly, 0<h<=2)
[6b] spot value h=1: closed form 1  vs h^2 = 1

[7] hence for measurable D subset S with ANGULAR LEBESGUE measure |D| (radians) and 0<h<=2:
    int_{K_h} |Jac| = |D| * h^2 exactly, where K_h = {(e,v): e in D, |e+v|<=h}.
    G(e,v)=x+e+v is at most 2-to-1 (fibre of w != 0 is {e,w-e}, |e|=|w-e|=1: two points),
    so |G(K_h)| >= |D| h^2/2, while G(K_h) subset B(x,h) with |B(x,h)| = pi h^2.
    Fraction of the ball B(x,h) on which (5.2) forbids colour i: >= |D|/(2 pi).
    exact shadow bound: |A_i cap B(x,h)| <= pi h^2 - |D| h^2/2  =>  limsup dens <= 1 - |D|/(2pi)
    (with the paper's normalised sigma = dtheta/2pi this reads 1 - sigma(D)/(2pi)... no:
     sigma(D) = |D|/2pi, so the bound is limsup dens <= 1 - sigma(D)/2 = 1 - |D|/(4pi).
     Use radians throughout; sigma(D) = |D|/(2pi).
    check |D|=pi/3: forbidden fraction = 1/6 = 1/6
