[9] COUNTERMODEL M1: x=0, i=1, c=1 on B(0,2), c=0 on R^2\B(0,2).
    - Lebesgue measurable (Borel, two open/closed pieces).
    - x=0 is a density-1 point of A_1: B(0,1) subset A_1, dens = 1.  c(x)=1.
    - sampling (y_n,r_n)=(0,1) constant, permitted by Definition 5.1.
    - is (0,1) a TYPICAL circle sampling? need 0+e typical for a.e. e: |0*e|=1<2 so 0+e is
      an interior point of A_1, hence a density-1 point of its own class: TRUE FOR EVERY e.
    - therefore a_1(0+1*e) = 1 for EVERY e, so b == (1,0,0,0,0,0) in L^inf(S;R^6) lies in W_0,
      b_1(e)=1>0 for every e, so e in D_{0,1} for every e: P_0(e) contains 1 FOR ALL e.
    - Def 1.2 (weak measurable) FAILS: {(y,u): |u|=1, y,y+u in B(0,2)} contains B(0,1) x S,
      product measure pi*1 = pi > 0, so the Def-1.2 sum equals >= pi > 0, not 0.
    - Lemma 5.2(2) FAILS in M1: with e_0=(1,0), v=(0,1), point x+e_0+v=(1,1), |(1,1)|<2,
      so A_1-density there is 1, not 0. (Consistent: 5.2(2) needs Lemma 4.3.)
    - Lemma 4.3 FAILS in M1: (0.0,0.5) and (1.0,0.5) are unit apart, both interior, both colour 1.
    - M1 is not proper: any unit pair inside B(0,2) is monochromatic.
    - strengthening: c=1 on B(0,R_0) makes Lemma D fail at EVERY x with |x| < R_0-1,
      a set of measure pi (R_0-1)^2 which tends to infinity. So the failure set can be conull.
